# 7. FEATURE THREE: DAILY PLANNER

This feature must contain a real scheduling algorithm.

Do not simply sort tasks and display them as if they were intelligently scheduled.

### User availability

Allow users to configure:
- The date being planned.
- Available start time.
- Available end time.
- Available working duration.
- Break preferences.
- Optional unavailable periods.

Make the defaults sensible so that a first-time user can try the planner immediately.

### Scheduling considerations

The algorithm should consider:
- Task deadlines.
- Task priority.
- Remaining work duration.
- Available time.
- Existing completed work.
- Existing schedule constraints.
- Breaks.
- Task dependencies if implemented.

Use deterministic, transparent JavaScript logic.

The same inputs should produce predictable results.

### Required behavior

The planner must:
1. Generate a chronological daily schedule.
2. Allocate tasks within available time.
3. Avoid overlapping time blocks.
4. Respect unavailable periods.
5. Include breaks when configured.
6. Avoid scheduling completed tasks.
7. Use remaining work rather than blindly reusing original estimates.
8. Identify unscheduled tasks.
9. Identify tasks at risk of missing deadlines.
10. Explain why tasks could not be scheduled.
11. Allow the student to regenerate the plan.
12. Allow the student to review changes before replacing an existing plan.

Never allocate more work than the available time permits.

Never claim that all tasks fit when they do not.

### Scheduling priorities

Create a reasonable prioritization system that combines:
- Deadline urgency.
- Explicit task priority.
- Remaining work.
- Available scheduling windows.

Document the prioritization rules in the code and README.

Do not claim the algorithm is mathematically optimal unless that has actually been established.

### Scheduling conflicts

If six hours of work remain but the student has only three hours available, the planner must:
- Schedule a feasible subset.
- Identify the unscheduled work.
- Explain the overload.
- Recommend reasonable alternatives.

Do not silently discard tasks.

### Schedule display

Show:
- Start time.
- End time.
- Task title.
- Planned duration.
- Task priority.
- Relevant deadline information.
- Breaks or unavailable periods.
- Unscheduled tasks.

Provide a clear visual distinction between completed, upcoming, active, delayed, and unscheduled work.

